      "text": "Let's start by determining the form of \\( n \\). According to the problem, when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 1. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[\nn = 6k + 1\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. First, let's express \\( n + 2010 \\) in terms of \\( k \\):\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, so it does not affect the remainder when divided by 6. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. We calculate:\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\nThis means that:\n\\[\n2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nSo, we can rewrite \\( 6k + 2011 \\) as:\n\\[\n6k + 2011 \\equiv 6k + 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nSince \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, it has a remainder of 0 when divided by 6. Therefore:\n\\[\n6k + 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nThis means that the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
